{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a significant noble figure during the twelfth century who ruled as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He inherited his position after the assassination of his father Raymond II by the Hashashin sect. This event left the young Raymond as a minor and forced his mother Hodierna to act as regent until he reached adulthood. His early life was marked by political instability within the Crusader states and constant threats from Muslim neighbors. Raymond eventually came of age and took full control of his county while navigating the complex web of alliances that defined the Levant at that time.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nThe young count faced immediate challenges upon assuming power. The Hashashin had killed his father to protest policies that favored the Templars. Raymond had to secure his borders against Nur ad-Din of Aleppo while managing internal dissent. He spent years consolidating his authority over the coastal cities under his rule. His marriage to Eschiva of Tiberias united the powerful County of Tripoli with the principality of Galilee through her family connections. This union strengthened his political standing but also drew him deeper into the conflicts involving the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*Political Struggles in the Kingdom*\nRaymond served as regent for the young Baldwin IV after the king's uncle Amalric died. The leper king was unable to rule effectively so Raymond governed the realm during a critical period. He held the title of regent from 1174 until 1176 when he was replaced by Raymond of Antioch. Tensions rose between Raymond and the faction supporting Guy of Lusignan. The count opposed Guy because he believed the inexperienced noble would ruin the kingdom through rash military decisions. Raymond advocated for a defensive strategy against Saladin rather than engaging in risky field battles.\n\n*The 